<title>YUGOSLAVIA: Yugoslav January-May tourism down 15.9 pct on '96.</title>
<headline>Yugoslav January-May tourism down 15.9 pct on '96.</headline>
<dateline>BELGRADE 1997-07-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Federal Statistics Office reported the following economic indicator details.</p>
<p> YUGOSLAV TOURISM	    Jan-May 97     Percent Change</p>
<p>						  (index base Jan-May 96)</p>
<p> Total tourists		 899,227	     -15.9</p>
<p> Yugoslav			 796,407	     -17.0</p>
<p> Ex-Yugoslav republics	 49,953	     - 5.7</p>
<p> Foreign			   52,867	     - 5.4</p>
<p> Overnights		   3,129,213	     -14.0</p>
<p>NOTES -- Most foreign visitors came from Italy (7,098), followed by Germany (4,584), Bulgaria (4,189), Greece (4,125), the Russian Federation (3,503) and Austria (3,264).</p>
<p>According to National Bank of Yugoslavia data, $12.6 million of hard currency income from tourism was registered in the January-May 1997 period, or 15.4 percent less than in the same five-month period last year.</p>
<p>Figures refer to the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, comprising Serbia and Montenegro.</p>
<p>Former Yugoslav republics refers to Bosnia, Croatia, Macedonia and Slovenia.</p>
<p>-- Amra Kevic, Belgrade newsroom +381 11 311 4305</p>
